From 9d541a2ba4ef9db06fba6f76a701084600a60d17 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 09:56:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: rockchip: rkisp2: Add the ISPP pipe mode

The RV1126 ISPP consumes the ISP output through a pool of buffers that
the ISPP owns: the mainpath DMA writes every frame, as a semi-planar
YUV422 picture plus a gain map, into the pool and the ISPP reads it from
there. The pool bases are physical addresses, the same scheme as the raw
readback, so they are mapped onto themselves in the ISP IOMMU domain for
the lifetime of the VICAP feed.

Add the output pad the ISPP links to, which carries the whole frame as
YUYV8_2X8, its width rounded down to a multiple of 16 pixels: the ISP
2.0 mainpath writes contiguous lines of the picture width and the ISPP
reads the pool with a 16 byte aligned stride, so the dual crop cuts
the width to what both agree on and the mainpath resizer is bypassed.
The params crop block leaves the mainpath crop and resizer alone while
the pipe is armed or running, as it would otherwise program them for
the idle mainpath capture node and its format. The pad is full range
and takes the range through the CSC flag of the format like the
mainpath pad; the colour space matrix of the ISP follows the pad that
feeds the pool. The ISP publishes a private subdev at its own device
node when its output port is connected; the ISPP matches it through an
async notifier, passes the pool with the subdev core ioctl operation
before the stream starts and gets the input format and the stream
control through it.

The ISP rotates the pool buffers: the base of the next buffer is written
at the frame start and latched by the auto update at the mainpath frame
end, which hands the completed buffer to the ISPP through a callback of
the pipe configuration, together with the ISP frame sequence so that
the ISPP output and the ISP statistics of one exposure share a number;
the ISPP returns the buffer with RKISP2_ISPP_CMD_PUT. A frame is dropped
when no buffer is free. The quick mode, in which the hardware alternates
two pool buffers and pipes the output line by line, only tracks the
frame buffer compression mode of the mainpath and is left selectable
for it.

The pipe stop runs after the readback drain and before the ISP stop:
it waits for the mainpath frame end of the last frame the ISP started,
disables the mainpath and the gain map writer and latches that state
with the memory interface soft update, as the ISP stop masks the
interrupts and resets the engines and the ISPP frees the pool right
after. The mainpath frame end is not fed to the capture handler while
the pipe runs, as that would re-target the write base to a capture
buffer. The pipe and the mainpath capture are exclusive. The stream on
takes a runtime PM reference so the pipe keeps the ISP powered without
a capture device, and the ISP 2.0 clock control ungates the memory
interface write engines the mainpath needs.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
